---
// The crawler page. OPPORTUNITY_INGESTION.md §2.1 rule 3 requires the crawler to
// "identify honestly" with a User-Agent pointing here, and rule 8 requires a
// published takedown process with a 48-hour SLA.
//
// Written for the person who found our User-Agent in their access log at 2am and
// wants to know what it is and how to make it stop. Everything that person needs is
// above the fold: who we are, what we take, and the one address that stops it.

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BRAND, CONTACT, CRAWLER_USER_AGENT } from "@mbele/config";
---

<Base
  title="About our crawler"
  description={`What ${BRAND.name}Bot does, how it behaves, and how to make it stop.`}
  canonical="/bot"
>
  <article class="max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-title font-bold tracking-[-0.015em]">About our crawler</h1>

    <p class="mt-3 text-dense">
      If you found this page from your server logs: we run a small crawler that collects published
      details of grants, scholarships, competitions and similar opportunities relevant to people in
      Africa, so they can be found in one place. It identifies itself as:
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 select-all overflow-x-auto rounded-row bg-sunken px-3 py-2 font-mono text-dense">
      {CRAWLER_USER_AGENT}
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-dense">
      One exception, so that your logs make sense. Some sites answer that user-agent with a bot
      challenge rather than a page. Where that happens we load the page once in an ordinary
      headless browser, which sends a normal Chrome user-agent because the challenge reads the
      user-agent to decide. Those requests are still ours, and they still say so — in two headers
      you can grep for and block on:
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 select-all overflow-x-auto rounded-row bg-sunken px-3 py-2 font-mono text-dense">
      X-Crawled-By: {CRAWLER_USER_AGENT}<br />From: {CONTACT.takedown}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      It is the same crawler under the same rules: robots.txt first, one request per ten seconds,
      and never past a login or a paywall. If you disallow us in
      <code class="font-mono">robots.txt</code>, that path stops too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">To make it stop</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.takedown}`} class="text-brand">{CONTACT.takedown}</a> and
      we stop within 48 hours. No justification needed, no argument, no negotiation. You can also
      disallow it in <code class="font-mono">robots.txt</code> and it will stop on its next check.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      If you want an opportunity of yours removed from the site as well, say so in the same email
      and it comes down in the same 48 hours.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">How it behaves</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-dense">
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">It honours robots.txt</strong> on every fetch, and records when
        it last checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">It never signs in.</strong> It does not create accounts, does
        not log in, and does not go around a paywall or any other authentication. If content needs an
        account to see, we do not have it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">At most one request every ten seconds</strong> per host. It
        honours <code class="font-mono">Retry-After</code>, and backs off on 429 and 503.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">It asks before it takes.</strong> Conditional requests with
        <code class="font-mono">ETag</code> and <code class="font-mono">If-Modified-Since</code>, so
        an unchanged page costs you almost nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">It stores facts, not your writing.</strong> Deadlines,
        eligibility rules, dates and links are facts about the world. Your prose is yours. Where we
        need to quote an eligibility requirement we quote it exactly and attribute it, because
        showing the reader your own words is the honest way to explain a rule.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">Summaries are written in our own words</strong> and checked
        against your text automatically — any overlap of eight consecutive words is rejected before
        it can be published.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">Every opportunity links back to your page.</strong> Always, on
        the page a reader actually looks at, not buried in a credits list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold">If your terms restrict automated access</strong>, we either use
        your RSS feed or we do not collect from you at all.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">Why we do it this way</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      Opportunities for African builders are scattered across hundreds of sites, and the people who
      need them most are the least able to spend data finding them. Collecting the facts in one place
      is worth doing. Taking your content to do it is not, so we do not.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 text-micro text-ink-2">
      Questions, complaints, or an offer of a better feed:
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.takedown}`} class="text-brand">{CONTACT.takedown}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
